$DIR/main.p:13:2: cannot store the function literal in arr[0], which outlives x
    A function literal that uses variables of the function around it refers to
    those variables where they live, in mk's frame (x is declared at main.p:11).
    When mk returns, x's memory is reclaimed, but arr[0] may be on the heap and
    live longer, so it would be left pointing at reclaimed memory.
    Go would move this memory to the heap, and its garbage collector would free
    it later. Plutonium has no garbage collector and never moves memory to the
    heap behind the program's back, so that every allocation is visible in the
    code.
    Store a value that carries its own state instead of the function literal,
    such as a struct with a method, or make x a package variable if it must
    outlive mk.
    (see "Views" in the Plutonium spec, section 18.3)
    (run "pluto explain E3002" for a longer explanation)

$DIR/main.p:15:12: cannot return V{...} as an interface: the interface would hold a copy of it in mk's frame
    Converting a value larger than one word to an interface puts a copy of the
    value in the frame of the function that converts it (here mk, at main.p:15),
    and the interface points at that copy. That frame is reclaimed when mk
    returns, so the interface would point at memory that no longer holds the
    value.
    Go would move this memory to the heap, and its garbage collector would free
    it later. Plutonium has no garbage collector and never moves memory to the
    heap behind the program's back, so that every allocation is visible in the
    code.
    Return a pointer to memory that outlives mk, such as a field of a
    longer-lived value:
        p.err = V{...}
        return &p.err
    or return a value that fits in one word, such as an error code.
    (see "Views" in the Plutonium spec, section 18.3)
    (run "pluto explain E3001" for a longer explanation)
